## Summary

Implemented the allergies exercise in `/app` (no `CMakeLists.txt` changes needed).

### `allergies.h`
Declares `allergies::allergy_test` with an `unsigned int` score constructor and the two query methods the tests use, both `const` so they work on the temporaries the tests construct:

```cpp
class allergy_test {
   public:
    explicit allergy_test(unsigned int score);
    bool is_allergic_to(const std::string& allergen) const;
    std::unordered_set<std::string> get_allergies() const;
   private:
    unsigned int score_;
};
```

### `allergies.cpp`
A table of the eight tested allergens with their bit values. The constructor masks the score with `255u`, so components scoring 256, 512, … are ignored as required. `is_allergic_to` matches by name, returns `false` for unknown names, and tests the corresponding bit; `get_allergies` `emplace`s each set bit into an `std::unordered_set<std::string>` and returns it.

### Verification (formal run via `run_tests`)
The public CMake command can't execute in this sandbox because no build tool is installed (`make`/`ninja` both absent → `CMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM is not set` at configure time). I therefore drove the real build+test through `run_tests`: a pytest case compiles the unmodified `allergies_test.cpp`, `allergies.cpp` and `test/tests-main.cpp` with the same flags CMake uses (`-std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -DEXERCISM_RUN_ALL_TESTS`) and runs the resulting Catch2 binary.

- Build: clean under `-Werror` (no warnings from my code).
- Test binary: `All tests passed (50 assertions in 50 test cases)`, exit 0.
- Extra behaviour probe through the public interface: score `257` → allergic to `eggs` only (`true`, `cats` false, set size 1) — higher bits ignored.
- `pytest -q` → **1 passed**.

Workspace contains only source files; temporary build directories were removed.
